{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791297211267,"sessionID":"ses_eee5de42effeNVlx7deoLtOguB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_01a111a248e771e1a5badfa1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/dsflash-max__prose__r1__1791297198/work/rewritten.txt","content":"Last quarter, the fleet daemon moved to v4.12.0. The upgrade consolidated the routing table from several scattered files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The daemon reads this file once at startup and caches it in memory. The cache stays until the process restarts or the supervisor sends a reload signal. Operators once edited the old files directly. These operators should validate any change first with the reload command and the --dry-run flag. A malformed entry that escapes detection would make every worker in the pool fall back to the default route. The incident review found that this fallback cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status when an entry fails to parse. A team can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort. The upgrade automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema. It dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged warnings for each one.\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/dsflash-max__prose__r1__1791297198/work/rewritten.txt","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/dsflash-max__prose__r1__1791297198/work/rewritten.txt","time":{"start":1791297211254,"end":1791297211259}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me rewrite this paragraph. Rules:\n\n1. Every sentence ≤ 20 words (whitespace-separated tokens; sentence ends with . ! ?).\n2. No passive constructions: no \"was\", \"were\", or \"been\" followed (optionally after one -ly adverb) by a word ending in \"ed\".\n3. Keep exact tokens: /etc/fleetd/routes.toml, --dry-run, v4.12.0, $0.35\n4. Every fact preserved. Plain prose, no headings/bullets.\n\nFacts to keep:\n- Fleet daemon upgraded to v4.12.0 last quarter.\n- Routing table previously stored in several scattered files; consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\n- That file is read once at startup, then cached in memory until process restart or reload signal from supervisor.\n- Operators accustomed to editing old files directly advised: any change should first be validated by running the reload command with --dry-run flag.\n- Malformed entry not caught before reload would cause every worker in pool to fall back to default route.\n- Fallback found during incident review to cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\n- Reload command prints a diff of routes that would change; exits nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed; can be wired into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\n- Older entries written for previous schema automatically migrated.\n- Entries referencing retired regions dropped and logged as warnings.\n\nNeed to avoid passive constructions. Passive pattern: \"was|were|been\" + optional one -ly adverb + word ending in \"ed\".\n\nSo avoid \"was consolidated\", \"was read\", \"were advised\", \"was found\", \"were written\", \"were dropped\", \"was caught\", \"was upgraded\", \"had previously been stored\".\n\nRewrite actively:\n\nSentence 1: \"Last quarter, the fleet daemon moved to v4.12.0.\" ≤20 words. Active. Good.\n\nSentence 2: \"The upgrade consolidated the routing table from several scattered files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\" Count: The(1) upgrade(2) consolidated(3) the(4) routing(5) table(6) from(7) several(8) scattered(9) files(10) into(11) one(12) file(13) at(14) /etc/fleetd/routes.toml(15). 15 words. Active. Good.\n\nSentence 3: \"The daemon reads this file once at startup and caches it in memory.\" Count: The(1) daemon(2) reads(3) this(4) file(5) once(6) at(7) startup(8) and(9) caches(10) it(11) in(12) memory(13). Good.\n\nSentence 4: \"The cache stays until the process restarts or the supervisor sends a reload signal.\" Count: The(1) cache(2) stays(3) until(4) the(5) process(6) restarts(7) or(8) the(9) supervisor(10) sends(11) a(12) reload(13) signal(14). Good. Active (stays is not passive; \"restarts\" fine).\n\nSentence 5: \"Operators had grown used to editing the old files directly.\" Hmm, \"used\" ends in \"ed\" but no was/were/been before it (had grown used — \"grown\" then \"used\"? Pattern requires was/were/been. \"had grown used\" has \"grown\" not was/were/been. But safer: \"Operators once edited the old files directly.\" Good, active. Count: Operators(1) once(2) edited(3) the(4) old(5) files(6) directly(7). Good.\n\nSentence 6: \"These operators should validate any change first with the reload command and the --dry-run flag.\" Count: These(1) operators(2) should(3) validate(4) any(5) change(6) first(7) with(8) the(9) reload(10) command(11) and(12) the(13) --dry-run(14) flag(15). Good. Active.\n\nSentence 7: \"A malformed entry that escapes detection would make every worker in the pool fall back to the default route.\" Count: A(1) malformed(2) entry(3) that(4) escapes(5) detection(6) would(7) make(8) every(9) worker(10) in(11) the(12) pool(13) fall(14) back(15) to(16) the(17) default(18) route(19). 19 words. Good.\n\nSentence 8: \"The incident review found that this fallback cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" Count: The(1) incident(2) review(3) found(4) that(5) this(6) fallback(7) cost(8) roughly(9) $0.35(10) per(11) thousand(12) requests(13) in(14) extra(15) upstream(16) charges(17). Good.\n\nSentence 9: \"The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change.\" Count: The(1) reload(2) command(3) prints(4) a(5) diff(6) of(7) the(8) routes(9) that(10) would(11) change(12). Good.\n\nSentence 10: \"It exits with a nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed.\" Count: It(1) exits(2) with(3) a(4) nonzero(5) status(6) if(7) any(8) entry(9) cannot(10) be(11) parsed(12). \"be parsed\" — passive? Rule only checks was/were/been. \"be\" isn't in the list. Fine per script. But to be safe and simple: \"It exits with a nonzero status when an entry fails to parse.\" Count: It(1) exits(2) with(3) a(4) nonzero(5) status(6) when(7) an(8) entry(9) fails(10) to(11) parse(12). Good, fully active.\n\nSentence 11: \"A team can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\" Count: A(1) team(2) can(3) wire(4) it(5) into(6) a(7) pre-commit(8) hook(9) without(10) much(11) effort(12). Good.\n\nSentence 12: \"The upgrade automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema.\" Count: The(1) upgrade(2) automatically(3) migrated(4) older(5) entries(6) written(7) for(8) the(9) previous(10) schema(11). Good.\n\nSentence 13: \"It dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged warnings for each one.\" Count: It(1) dropped(2) entries(3) that(4) referenced(5) retired(6) regions(7) and(8) logged(9) warnings(10) for(11) each(12) one(13). Good.\n\nCheck rule 2 across text: scan for was/were/been. I have none of those words at all. Let me verify: \"was\" — no. \"were\" — no. \"been\" — no. \n\nBut wait: \"written\" in sentence 12 — preceded by \"entries written for\" — no was/were/been. Fine.\n\nWords containing \"was\"? No. Careful: no token equal to was/were/been.\n\nCheck exact tokens: /etc/fleetd/routes.toml ✓, --dry-run ✓, v4.12.0 ✓, $0.35 ✓.\n\nNo headings, no bullets. Plain prose paragraph.\n\nFacts: all kept. \"read once at startup and cached in memory\" ✓ via sentence 3. \"until the process is restarted or a reload signal is received from the supervisor\" ✓ via sentence 4 (cache stays until process restarts or supervisor sends reload signal).\n\nShould I keep \"had previously been stored in several scattered files\" — sentence 2 covers scattered files. Good.\n\nWrite to rewritten.txt. Reply DONE only. Also \"Do not modify source.txt\" — we don't.\n\nOne consideration: \"The upgrade consolidated...\" — active voice. Good.\n\nWrite the file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_111a248f8001LYM5bP34zLLWXO","sessionID":"ses_eee5de42effeNVlx7deoLtOguB","messageID":"msg_111a22e21001Y2GcvVqbWb6d65"}}
